"""Writes the narration takes to voice_text/<section>.txt (VibeVoice-friendly: plain words, numbers spelled out)."""
from pathlib import Path

T = {
    "02": "Last time, I had an AI teach itself to speedrun Zelda. It built its own tools, learned the game from scratch, and got faster run after run. And when that video went up, a whole bunch of you asked for the same thing. Battletoads. So, that's what we did. And hey, this video exists because of your comments, so keep them coming, and subscribe if you want to see what it takes on next.",
    "03": "Now, if you grew up with a Nintendo, you know Battletoads. It's one of the hardest games ever made, and the Turbo Tunnel alone ended a lot of childhoods. The fastest human run of this game is twelve minutes, twenty nine point two five seconds, by a runner named Jay Cee. And the AI has to play by the same rules a human does. The hidden warps in levels one, four and six are allowed, so to answer the question, yes, warps. No credits warp, which is a memory corruption trick. No pressing left and right at the same time, no turbo, and the clock starts the moment the toad lands. So, two goals. First, just beat the game. Then, beat twelve twenty nine.",
    "04": "Before we jump in, here's how to read the screen. On the left is the actual run. The boxes come straight out of the game's own memory, so it knows where the toad is and how much health every enemy has left. On the right is what it's doing right now, and why. Below that is the controller, the exact buttons it's holding on every single frame. Then the clock, with the record's pace in gold and the AI in green. Under that is what it's reading out of memory. And at the bottom is the token counter. That's how much AI thinking it took to figure out the part you're watching.",
    "05": "Day one. The first thing Claude did was build itself a way into the game. It wrote a little bridge into the emulator, so it could step forward one frame at a time, save the game, load it back, and read the memory. And it started blind. It didn't know where anything was. So it ran experiments. What does every button do on the title screen? What's the earliest frame you can press start and have it actually count? Then it went looking for the toad. Press right, and watch which numbers in memory go up. Jump, and see which one changes. Take a hit, and see what drops. That's how it built its map of the game, one byte at a time. Then came research day. It read the speedrun rules, the notes from people who have made tool assisted runs of this game, and a fan's breakdown of the game's code. And on level one, it found the hidden warp sitting right there in memory. Level one takes under five seconds. And here's the thing to understand about how this works. Every one of those red lines is a different way to make the same jump, and most of them miss. The AI doesn't play the game. It writes programs that search through thousands of possibilities like this, keeps the one that works, and then a script plays it back.",
    "06": "So, goal one. Just beat it. And at first, honestly, it couldn't. The rats in level three beat it up over and over. But it kept going. The Turbo Tunnel, the level everybody remembers, it rode the whole thing. Walls coming at twelve pixels a frame, and the last section was solved completely by search. Level four, the Arctic Caverns. Ice blocks, snowmen, and a pit after checkpoint five that ate most of an afternoon. Karnath's Lair, where the snakes are the platforms. You ride them up and catch the warp. The Terra Tubes. Swimming is about three times slower than walking, and there's a current that kept dragging the search to the bottom, until it followed the line from one of those tool assisted runs. The Rat Race. Kick a falling rat the right way, and the whole level just ends. That's allowed. Clinger Winger. Keep your speed up, or the Buzzball catches you. It did that one in a single evening. Then the tower, and the Dark Queen. And there were plenty of failures along the way. Searches that ran for hours and found nothing. Toads falling off the tower over and over. At one point it even shut down its own searches by accident. But on day five, the game was beaten. Eighteen minutes and four seconds. The record is twelve twenty nine. So, a long way to go.",
    "07": "Goal two. Shave the time. First, it measured every level against the record to see where the time was going, and three levels were most of it. The Turbo Tunnel, level four, and the tower. The Turbo Tunnel was the big one, because the record skips almost the whole level. You crash the bike into a gap before the first checkpoint. When you respawn, the game is supposed to load the level from your last checkpoint, but there isn't one yet. So it starts reading the game's own variables as if they were level data. It walks through memory eleven bytes at a time, until it lands on a one twenty seven, and one twenty seven means level over. The AI read the game's code to understand exactly why that works, then rebuilt it frame by frame. That alone was worth over two minutes. Then the small stuff. Every level starts on an intro page, and pressing a button skips it. That's about twenty seconds over the whole run. And then there's chaos. Change anything early, and the game's random numbers change for everything after it. Enemies move differently. Bosses attack differently. So every improvement early in the run meant re-fitting every level after it. The tower is where it kept losing time. It was always waiting, on platforms, on wind, on enemies. But the record holder has to wait there too, so the waiting wasn't really the problem. It was how you wait. So it studied one of those tool assisted runs frame by frame, and copied how it handles every wait. The final run climbs that tower almost half a minute faster than the first one. And the clock kept dropping. Twelve fifty eight. Twelve thirty seven. Twelve thirty four. Twelve thirty one. Then twelve twenty eight point six. The record was beaten. Honestly, I almost stopped right there. But winning by a fraction of a second leaves room for people to say it didn't really happen. So we kept going. Twelve twenty three point three nine. Almost six seconds under the record. And every run gets checked. It replays from power on, twice, and both replays have to end with the exact same memory. It even caught one banned up plus down press hiding in the inputs, took it out, re-ran everything, and got the exact same result.",
    "08": "So, is this a T A S? Yes. A tool assisted speedrun is built on an emulator with save states, and then played back as a file of button presses. That's exactly what this is. The difference is how it was built. Most of them are made by people, frame by frame, sometimes over months. This one was built by an AI that wrote its own search tools, in twelve days, and it stuck to the human rules the whole way.",
    "09": "And here's what it took. Twelve days. Five thousand three hundred and eighty eight replies from Claude. About three billion tokens, which works out to around thirteen hundred dollars at A P I prices. And almost all of that is the AI re-reading its own notes, and everything it has done so far, every time it takes a step. Alright. Here's the whole run, from power on, to the Dark Queen.",
    "10a": "Under five seconds, and into the warp.",
    "10b": "Watch the gap. This is the skip.",
    "10c": "And there's the rat boot.",
    "10d": "The tower. Pump the spring, and go.",
    "10e": "And that's the Dark Queen.",
    "11": "Huge thanks to Jay Cee for the record to chase, to J C five eight three for the run notes, and to everyone who has made tool assisted runs of this game. This wouldn't exist without you. So, what should it play next? Zelda two? Contra? Ninja Turtles? Let me know in the comments. And if you know Battletoads, tell me what it did wrong. Where would you have saved time? Thanks for watching.",
}

if __name__ == "__main__":
    d = Path(__file__).parent / "voice_text"
    d.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    for k, v in T.items():
        (d / f"{k}.txt").write_text(v + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    print(sum(len(v.split()) for v in T.values()), "words in", len(T), "takes")
